# The threat model of a course platform

### Who can hurt a Tutors course, and how

Tutors Ladder, rung 6 (Guarded), talk 1

---

## Why a course platform needs a threat model

- Tutors turns **someone else's Markdown** into pages that thousands of students open
- A course folder is code-adjacent: it is built by a generator, on a laptop or in CI
- Courses can be **private** (`auth: 1`) and readers can sign in with GitHub
- Every build installs hundreds of packages from the npm registry

A threat model answers four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what are we doing about it,
and did we do a good enough job? (the Threat Modeling Manifesto's four questions)

---

## The assets

| Asset | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| Readers' browsers | script in a course page runs with the reader's session |
| The author's machine and CI | the generator reads files; a course can ask it to read the wrong ones |
| Private course content | `auth: 1` courses are for enrolled readers only |
| Student data | upstream stores activity, calendars and submissions in Supabase |
| The release | what `pnpm install` brings in ships to every user |

---

## The actors

| Actor | Trusted to | Not trusted to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A **course author** | write course content | write HTML or script that runs in readers' browsers |
| A **reader** (anonymous or a student) | read what the policy allows | see private courses, or other students' data |
| A **tutor** (upstream: educator) | lock content, see analytics for their course | do the same for another course |
| A **package maintainer** upstream of us | publish a version | change a version we already pinned |

The author is the surprising one. A course can be forked, copied from a colleague, or written by a student.

---

## Trust boundaries

```
course folder ──gen──► tutors.json ──lite/reader──► HTML in a browser
   (author)         (CI or laptop)                   (reader's session)

npm registry ──pnpm install──► node_modules ──build──► release
```

Each arrow crosses a boundary. Something untrusted goes in on the left; something trusted comes out on the
right. Every defence in this topic sits on one of these arrows.

---

## Threat 1: hostile course content (stored XSS)

```markdown
Click [the reading list](javascript:alert%28document.domain%29) to start.

<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
```

- Stored, because the payload lives in the course and runs for **every** reader
- Upstream's answer: DOMPurify on rendered HTML
  (`packages/svelte/ui-primitives/src/utils/sanitize.ts`), and course-rules V016 warns about
  `<script>`, `on...=` and `javascript:` in slides (`STRIPPED` in `packages/jsr/course-rules/src/rich.ts`)

---

## Threat 2: the course reads outside its folder

```bash
ln -s ~/.ssh/config course.md      # or a page linking ../../../answers/solutions.md
```

- The generator runs with **your** permissions. A link can make it read, and publish, any file you can read.
- Upstream rule V021 (`checkPortable` in `packages/jsr/course-rules/src/warnings.ts`) warns when a page shows or
  links to files outside its own folder. Upstream calls it portability; it is also containment.
- Rung 3 already skips links in folder listings, but follows `course.md` if it is one. Lab 07 shows it.

---

## Threat 3: readers who should not read

From `guides/RBAC.md` upstream:

| `auth` in `properties.yaml` | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Public, no authentication required |
| `1` | Must authenticate via GitHub. Whitelist and enrollment checks apply. |

- A **static** copy of a private course has no sign-in at all
- Upstream's data layer had its own gap: `supabase/migrations/20260924_enable_rls_public_tables.sql` records
  that 14 tables had Row-Level Security off, "so the anon key every browser holds could read, change,
  delete and truncate them"

---

## Threat 4: the supply chain

- A dependency from a **git URL or tarball** can change under the same name
- A **missing lockfile** means each install may resolve something new
- A **known advisory** in a dependency ships with the release
- A **workflow** with broad token permissions can be turned against the repository

Upstream answers each: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and a dependency-audit job in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, CodeQL, zizmor, OpenSSF Scorecard and an OpenVEX file (talk 3).

---

## What upstream says it does: SECURITY.md

- Report privately to **security@tutors.dev**; acknowledgement within 48 hours, triage within 7 days, a
  fix for critical issues aimed at within 30 days, a 90-day coordinated-disclosure window
- In scope: the four apps (reader, catalogue, live, time), `packages/`, `services/`, CI/CD, deployment config
- Measures it lists: DOMPurify against XSS, environment validation, security headers, dependency auditing

---

## A claim is not a check

`SECURITY.md` says all deployed apps "enforce security headers including Content-Security-Policy".
`tests/security/known-response-gaps.txt`, which may only shrink, says:

```
# No app sends a Content-Security-Policy. Writing one needs an inventory of what the
# reader loads (Iconify, KaTeX, Mermaid, Adobe PDF embed, YouTube/Panopto/Vimeo frames,
# Supabase realtime) and a report-only rollout, so it is not guessed here.
missing-header: reader: Content-Security-Policy
```

The test is the truth (L3). The prose drifted (L2). That is the Assertion Ladder's point, in security.

---

## Upstream's own posture: docs/security

`docs/security/scorecard-baseline.md`, captured 2026-08-31 with Scorecard v5.5.0:

- **Aggregate 6.4 / 10**
- 10/10: Pinned-Dependencies, Token-Permissions, Dangerous-Workflow, Security-Policy, Fuzzing
- Low: Code-Review 3, SAST 1, Branch-Protection 0, CII-Best-Practices 0
- Remediations in order: branch protection, CodeQL on every commit, review before merge, triage advisories

A baseline that names its own gaps is a good security document.

---

## The rung 6 threat table

| Threat | Rung 6 defence |
| --- | --- |
| Script in course content | escaping, a URL allow-list, a CSP with no script |
| Reading outside the course | `containedPath` in every adapter; V021 warnings |
| A private course published openly | an RBAC policy; `--private`; pages marked |
| A bad dependency | lockfile required, registry sources only, audit with expiring allowances |

Out of scope for this rung: response headers, sessions and cookies, the database. They are the Summit's.

---

## Summary

- Name the assets, the actors and the boundaries before writing a defence
- In Tutors the **author is untrusted** and the **generator runs as you**
- Upstream documents its policy (`SECURITY.md`) and its posture (`docs/security`); its tests show where the
  two differ
- Next: the defences, in depth

